nstead of applying them in the WHERE clause.\n\nFor example, the condition `ss_item_sk = i_item_sk` is moved from the WHERE clause to the ON clause of the first INNER JOIN operation. Similarly, the condition `ws_item_sk = ss_item_sk` is also moved to the ON clause of the second INNER JOIN operation.\n\nThe filter conditions on the date_dim table are also applied in the rewritten query. The condition `d2.d_date between d1.d_date and (d1.d_date + interval '30' day)` is applied as a filter on the first join operation, and the condition `ss_sold_date_sk = d1.d_date_sk` is moved to the ON clause of the same join operation.\n\nStep 3: Matching cases in the query rewrite rule:\n\nThe rewritten query matches Case 1 of the query rewrite rule. The conditions in the WHERE clause are logically associated only with columns from one side of the join, and therefore can be applied as filters on each join operation instead of applying them in the WHERE clause.\n\nStep 4: Explanation of the query rewrite process:\n\nThe rewritten query applies the filter conditions to each join operation instead of applying them in the WHERE clause. This is done by moving the conditions from the WHERE clause to the ON clause or as a filter on each join operation.\n\nFor example, the condition `ss_item_sk = i_item_sk` is moved from the WHERE clause to the ON clause of the first INNER JOIN operation. This allows the query to reduce the size of the datasets before the join occurs, which can improve performance.\n\nSimilarly, the condition `ws_item_sk = ss_item_sk` is also moved to the ON clause of the second INNER JOIN operation. This ensures that only matching rows from both tables are joined together, which can further improve performance.\n\nThe filter conditions on the date_dim table are also applied in the rewritten query. The condition `d2.d_date between d1.d_date and (d1.d_date + interval '30' day)` is applied as a filter on the first join operation, and the condition `ss_sold_date_sk = d1.d_date_sk` is moved to the ON clause of the same join operation.\n\nOverall, the rewritten query applies the filter conditions to each join operation instead of applying them in the WHERE clause. This can improve performance by reducing the size of the datasets before the join occurs and ensuring that only matching rows from both tables are joined together.", 'time': 11.885809898376465}
